// Generated by scripts/restructure-by-construct.
// Per-construct DSL layout: every <kind>s.memql file in a domain
// holds every construct of that kind in the domain. The companion
// files cover the other construct kinds.

// THE DEFAULT ORDER IS LOCAL-STRONGEST, THEN AN APP, THEN FEDERATION
// (epic memql#5096, design D4).
//
// Every shipped policy below begins `fleet:*`, then `app:*`, then the vendor
// entries an author wrote. The three steps are three different kinds of cost,
// in increasing order:
//
//   fleet:*   a model on hardware the person already owns. Costs electricity.
//   app:*     a signed-in Claude Code or Codex on one of their machines. Costs
//             a subscription they already pay for; MemQL is not billed.
//   vendors   a metered key or workload-identity federation. Costs money, and
//             is therefore the hop the cost ceiling gates.
//
// `fleet:*` and `app:*` are WILDCARDS, and that is what makes this shippable
// as a default: a policy naming one model id would park on every fleet running
// something else, and which weights a person pulled is not knowable here. The
// wildcard resolves per call, strongest first, among the models that can serve
// THAT call.
//
// WHAT CHANGED, AND WHAT DID NOT. The local-models epic (memql#4676) made four
// seeded policies local-first with NO fallback, so an idle fleet PARKED. That
// was right when the only alternative to a laptop was a metered key: a chain
// reaching the cloud starts billing the moment a lid closes, silently, because
// nothing about a working reply says which vendor produced it. There is now a
// middle step that costs nothing extra, so the default falls through to it --
// and the FEDERATION hop, the one that does cost money, asks the cost ceiling
// before it is taken and refuses with `ceiling_reached` rather than spending
// past it. Work still parks; it parks when EVERY door is shut.
//
// PARK-NOT-FALLBACK STILL HOLDS FOR A PINNED POLICY. A policy that names one
// fleet model and authors no fallback refuses exactly as it did, and
// `consentedCloudFallback` still exists for a person's one-shot yes. What
// changed is the DEFAULT, not the rule.
//
// The four `local*` policies this file used to carry are DELETED. They were
// seeded and never named by anything -- design D6 required them wired to a
// purpose or removed, and the purposes they named are gone or covered: the
// planner loop was retired (memql#5052), there is no conductor in the engine,
// and suggest and embeddings reach the fleet through the chain below and
// through `fleet:` resolution on the embedding provider. A policy nothing
// names is not a default; it is a decoration that reads like one.

/// Default chat policy for non-operator agents. Local first, then a signed-in app on the person's
/// own machine, then Claude Sonnet 4.6, GPT-5.4 Pro as a cross-vendor fallback and Claude Haiku as
/// a tertiary safety net. The cloud entries stay Sonnet-class-or-better because a Mini-primary
/// produced too many hedged, un-cited replies (the model smoothed RAG'd specifics into generic
/// platitudes); use cheapestCapable for paths that legitimately need Mini-class speed and cost.
@primary("fleet:*")
@fallback("app:*")
@fallback("streamClaudeSonnet")
@fallback("stream54Pro")
@fallback("streamClaudeHaiku")
@maxLatencyMs(60000)
@preferredRole("assistant")
@preferredRole("specialist")
policy balancedChat { }

// ---- policies/v1/cheapestCapable.memql ----

/// Cheapest capable -- bulk suggestion + classification work where cost per call matters more than
/// model ceiling. GPT-5.4 Mini primary, Claude Haiku as the cross-vendor second, GPT-5.4 Nano as
/// the rock-bottom third.
@primary("fleet:*")
@fallback("app:*")
@fallback("stream54Mini")
@fallback("streamClaudeHaiku")
@fallback("stream54Nano")
@maxLatencyMs(15000)
policy cheapestCapable { }

// ---- policies/v1/fastCoding.memql ----

/// Fast coding -- cheap + quick for code generation and refactor assistance. Codex 5.3 primary
/// (coding-specialized), GPT-5.4 Mini as fallback, Claude Haiku as a cross-vendor last resort.
@primary("fleet:*")
@fallback("app:*")
@fallback("streamCodex53")
@fallback("stream54Mini")
@fallback("streamClaudeHaiku")
@maxLatencyMs(20000)
policy fastCoding { }

// ---- policies/v1/strongReasoning.memql ----

/// Strong reasoning -- for operator-enabled agents driving the UI and for complex tool-calling
/// choreography. Claude Sonnet primary, GPT-5.4 Pro as a second, Claude Opus as a third.
@primary("fleet:*")
@fallback("app:*")
@fallback("streamClaudeSonnet")
@fallback("stream54Pro")
@fallback("streamClaudeOpus")
@maxLatencyMs(60000)
@preferredRole("operator")
policy strongReasoning { }

// ---- policies/v1/backgroundExecution.memql ----

/// Background / batch execution lane -- CHEAP DEFAULT tier (memql#898). Routine background work
/// (e.g. produceArtifact file generation) does not need the strong interactive model, so the cloud
/// half of this chain is GPT-5.4 Mini with cross-vendor cheap fallbacks (Claude Haiku, then GPT-5.4
/// Nano). When the cheap tier gets stuck on a turn (repeated tool-call failures), the background
/// executor escalates mid-turn to backgroundEscalation. Kept separate from the interactive chat
/// policies (memql#897) so the batch tier is tuned independently of live chat; higher maxLatencyMs
/// because no human waits on its first token; pairs with the background si_guard rate bucket.
///
/// NO `app:*` STEP, and its absence is the design rather than an omission (epic memql#5096, D3).
/// This lane runs TOOL-CALLING turns, and an app door does not serve them: on a tool turn MemQL is
/// driving, and an app is an agent that drives itself -- it reaches MemQL's tools through MCP, in
/// the other direction. An `app:*` entry here would be one the router skips on every call, which
/// reads to a later author like a door that is always shut.
///
/// `fleet:*` DOES belong here: a local model that advertises `tools=1` serves this lane exactly as
/// a vendor does, which is what memql#5098 built.
@primary("fleet:*")
@fallback("stream54Mini")
@fallback("streamClaudeHaiku")
@fallback("stream54Nano")
@maxLatencyMs(120000)
policy backgroundExecution { }

// ---- policies/v1/backgroundEscalation.memql ----

/// Background lane ESCALATION tier (memql#898). The strong chain the background executor swaps to
/// when the cheap backgroundExecution tier gets stuck on a turn (an explicit stuck signal --
/// repeated rounds where every tool call errored, which usually means the cheap model is
/// mishandling the workbench/worker tool). Escalation happens at most once per turn and reuses the
/// in-progress message history, so it costs one stronger continuation, not a full re-run.
///
/// `fleet:*` leads here too, and that is not a contradiction of "escalate to something stronger":
/// the wildcard resolves to the STRONGEST eligible local model, and a fleet running a 70B is a
/// better escalation than a cloud Sonnet is a cheaper one. When the fleet cannot serve the turn --
/// no tool support, nothing awake -- the chain falls to Claude Sonnet and GPT-5.4 Pro exactly as
/// before. No `app:*` step, for the tool-turn reason stated on backgroundExecution.
@primary("fleet:*")
@fallback("streamClaudeSonnet")
@fallback("stream54Pro")
@maxLatencyMs(120000)
policy backgroundEscalation { }
